Description: Don't call destroy_completed on an already-destroyed notification actor.
 .
 Upstream 8.6.1 added an early return for the case where the bubble's
 slide-out transition is interrupted, because mutter has already destroyed
 the window actor and destroy_completed() then dereferences a freed
 MetaWindow (SIGSEGV in meta_window_get_buffer_rect).
 .
 The guard only fires when window_type is NOTIFICATION, which elementary's
 own bubbles never are: NotificationsClient calls ManagedClient.make_dock(),
 which rewrites _NET_WM_WINDOW_TYPE to _NET_WM_WINDOW_TYPE_DOCK on X11, so
 mutter reports DOCK. Dismissing a bubble still crashed gala.
 .
 An interrupted transition means the actor may be gone whatever the window
 type, so drop the type check.
--- gala-8.6.2+r3169+pkg135~ubuntu8.1.orig/src/WindowManager.vala
+++ gala-8.6.2+r3169+pkg135~ubuntu8.1/src/WindowManager.vala
@@ -1170,12 +1170,14 @@
 
                 default:
                     if (NotificationStack.is_notification (window)) {
-                        if (!(yield notification_stack.destroy_notification (actor)) && window.window_type == NOTIFICATION) {
+                        if (!(yield notification_stack.destroy_notification (actor))) {
                             /* This is a workaround for X11. On X11 notifications actually get the window type
                                notification which is according to mutter not allowed to have any destroy animations.
                                Therefore if the destroy animation didn't finish this means it was interrupted probably
                                because mutter destroyed the actor already so don't call destroy_completed
-                               because that would lead to a use after free. */
+                               because that would lead to a use after free.
+                               The window type is not checked: NotificationsClient rewrites elementary's own
+                               bubbles to DOCK, so they never match NOTIFICATION and would still crash. */
                             destroying.remove (actor);
                             return;
                         }
